1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,220
Imagine you stand outdoors at night and
look into the deepest reaches of space.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:10,080
How can light travel across 93
billion light-years if time

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,780
itself began only 13.8 billion years ago?

4
00:00:15,847 --> 00:00:20,500
Understanding this giant puzzle reveals
your true place in a shifting cosmic

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,300
landscape.

6
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:26,240
In this video, you will discover how
cosmic expansion turns a journey of

7
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:31,260
13.8 billion years into a
vast width of 93 billion

8
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:32,133
light-years.

9
00:00:32,589 --> 00:00:36,700
Nothing in physics can travel faster than
the speed of light through local space.

10
00:00:37,464 --> 00:00:41,180
If light has only been traveling
for 13.8 billion years,

11
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,800
a simple sum suggests our cosmic
horizon should be much smaller.

12
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:51,960
You might expect a sphere with a radius
of 13.8 billion light-years and a

13
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:54,959
width of 27.6 billion light-years.

14
00:00:55,910 --> 00:01:01,400
Instead, astronomical observations
reveal a diameter of 93

15
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,340
billion light-years.

16
00:01:03,778 --> 00:01:07,680
This contradiction seems to break
the most fundamental rules of motion.

17
00:01:09,585 --> 00:01:13,580
To solve this mystery, you must first
unlearn a common mental picture.

18
00:01:14,231 --> 00:01:19,300
Imagine an empty room where distant
objects race through motionless air like

19
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:20,340
speedboats on a lake.

20
00:01:21,045 --> 00:01:26,320
In this false picture, galaxies fly apart
through fixed and static space after a

21
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:27,240
central explosion.

22
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:30,620
If galaxies were simply
speeding through static space,

23
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:33,320
no galaxy could exceed the speed of light.

24
00:01:34,071 --> 00:01:39,400
Under that simple picture, light
leaving a galaxy 13.8 billion years ago

25
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:44,000
could only show us objects 13.8
billion light-years away today.

26
00:01:44,841 --> 00:01:50,220
However, astronomical measurements show
distant galaxies moving away from us at

27
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,760
recessional speeds proportional
to their distance.

28
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:58,980
In fact, very distant regions of
space expand away from us faster

29
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,220
than the speed of light.

30
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:06,100
How can distant galaxies recede faster than
light without violating physical laws?

31
00:02:07,348 --> 00:02:08,340
The answer is simple.

32
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:15,480
Speed limits in special relativity
apply only to objects moving through

33
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:18,820
local space, not to
the space between them.

34
00:02:20,479 --> 00:02:24,040
Imagine replacing the idea of
a fixed box with a flexible,

35
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:25,340
stretching fabric.

36
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:31,240
The expansion of the universe is an
intrinsic increase in distance between cosmic

37
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:32,520
objects over time.

38
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:38,180
This cosmic expansion does not mean the
universe is expanding into any surrounding

39
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:39,000
empty room.

40
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:44,780
Cosmologists describe physical distances
using a scale factor that grows

41
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:46,020
over cosmic time.

42
00:02:46,744 --> 00:02:48,600
As the cosmic scale factor grows,

43
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,300
the physical separation between distant
galaxies increases automatically.

44
00:02:54,381 --> 00:02:58,580
Galaxies themselves remain nearly at rest
relative to their local surroundings,

45
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:00,320
called comoving coordinates.

46
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:06,200
Yet the space between separated galaxies
grows continuously as time passes.

47
00:03:07,021 --> 00:03:11,540
Imagine drawing two marks on a flexible
sheet that is constantly pulled in every

48
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:12,340
direction.

49
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:15,760
The coordinate distance between
the two marks stays constant,

50
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:18,860
but the actual physical
ruler distance grows.

51
00:03:19,753 --> 00:03:24,300
Because space stretches, physical distances
today are much larger than physical

52
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:26,240
distances in the distant past.

53
00:03:28,016 --> 00:03:33,120
Imagine an ant crawling across a giant rubber
band while someone stretches that band

54
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:34,100
at the same time.

55
00:03:34,753 --> 00:03:36,800
The ant crawls forward at a steady speed,

56
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:39,960
but the path ahead and
behind keeps expanding.

57
00:03:40,793 --> 00:03:44,100
A light beam traveling through
space behaves just like that ant.

58
00:03:44,792 --> 00:03:48,940
Locally, light always travels through
space at its standard constant speed.

59
00:03:49,746 --> 00:03:53,500
But as light travels toward
us across billions of years,

60
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:57,640
the space it has already
crossed continues to stretch.

61
00:03:58,432 --> 00:04:01,160
When the ancient light from the
early universe was first emitted,

62
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:04,140
the source was actually very close to us.

63
00:04:04,768 --> 00:04:10,180
Scientists estimate that matter emitting
the cosmic microwave background was only 42

64
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:13,240
million light-years away
when it emitted that light.

65
00:04:13,989 --> 00:04:18,580
The light spent 13.8 billion years
traveling across space to reach Earth.

66
00:04:19,265 --> 00:04:22,380
During that long journey
of 13.8 billion years,

67
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:25,680
the space behind the light
expanded dramatically.

68
00:04:26,379 --> 00:04:31,740
Consequently, the matter that emitted those
photons is now located much farther away

69
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:32,540
from Earth today.

70
00:04:34,254 --> 00:04:39,040
Now the mathematical puzzle of cosmic
distances starts to make complete sense.

71
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:44,420
The light-travel distance equals the age
of the universe multiplied by the speed of

72
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:48,292
light, which is 13.8 billion light-years.

73
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:53,960
That value of 13.8 billion light-years
represents the journey time of light

74
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:56,528
moving through an expanding spacetime.

75
00:04:57,005 --> 00:05:02,220
However, cosmologists define proper
distance as the physical distance measured

76
00:05:02,220 --> 00:05:05,020
at one frozen moment of cosmic time.

77
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:09,860
In today's standard model, the current proper
distance to the edge of our observable

78
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:14,416
universe is about 46.5
billion light-years.

79
00:05:14,865 --> 00:05:19,760
This distance is also known as the
comoving radius of the observable region.

80
00:05:20,523 --> 00:05:23,660
The photon traveled
for 13.8 billion years,

81
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:28,580
but cosmic expansion stretched the
physical space behind it along the way.

82
00:05:29,699 --> 00:05:34,960
This means that 13.8 billion
years of light travel time

83
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:38,720
converts into a current physical radius of

84
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:42,099
46.5 billion light-years.

85
00:05:43,583 --> 00:05:46,060
To find the full size of
our observable cosmos,

86
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:48,500
you simply look in every direction.

87
00:05:49,179 --> 00:05:54,220
Light has had equal time to travel toward
Earth from all directions across the sky.

88
00:05:54,951 --> 00:05:59,640
Because of this symmetry, the observable
universe forms a vast sphere centered on

89
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:00,440
Earth.

90
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:06,000
The radius of this cosmic sphere is 46.5
billion light-years in any direction.

91
00:06:06,793 --> 00:06:09,680
To calculate the total diameter
of this spherical region,

92
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:11,060
you double the radius.

93
00:06:12,044 --> 00:06:17,180
Doubling 46.5 billion light-years
yields a current diameter of 93

94
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:18,738
billion light-years.

95
00:06:19,190 --> 00:06:24,240
That is why the observable universe is
93 billion light-years across today.

96
00:06:24,912 --> 00:06:29,700
In today's standard model, that means
there are more stars in our observable

97
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:33,820
sphere than grains of sand
on all Earth beaches.

98
00:06:35,859 --> 00:06:39,500
What lies beyond the edge of this
93 billion light-year boundary?

99
00:06:40,618 --> 00:06:43,120
Objects outside our
observable sphere exist,

100
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:47,620
but their light has not had enough time
to reach Earth since the Big Bang.

101
00:06:48,359 --> 00:06:51,500
The limit of what we can see is
called the particle horizon.

102
00:06:52,398 --> 00:06:55,120
The total size of the
entire universe is unknown,

103
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:57,720
and it may even be infinite in extent.

104
00:06:58,546 --> 00:07:03,920
Every location in space has its own unique
observable universe sphere centered on

105
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:04,720
itself.

106
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:08,820
In today's standard model, dark
energy accelerates expansion,

107
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:13,944
creating a future visibility
limit at 62 billion light-years.

108
00:07:14,396 --> 00:07:19,480
This means the number of galaxies we can
ever observe in the infinite future is only

109
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:22,500
2.36 times larger than today.

110
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:28,500
In summary, our observable universe is 93
billion light-years wide because space

111
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:32,860
expanded while light traveled
for 13.8 billion years.

112
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:36,240
As you ponder this
expanding cosmic fabric,

113
00:07:36,820 --> 00:07:40,821
consider how much of the cosmos will
forever remain beyond our sight.
